{"name":"Palomino Tan","hex":"#c2aa8d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"palomino","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A horse with a golden-colored coat and a white or cream-colored mane and tail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palomino"},{"term":"tan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["The trigonometric function tangent."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Tangale."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Palomino","blocks":[{"text":"Palomino is a genetic color in horses, consisting of a gold coat and white mane and tail; the degree of whiteness can vary from bright white to yellow. The palomino color derived from the breeding of Spanish horses in the United States. Genetically, the palomino color is created by a single allele of a dilution gene called the cream gene working on a \"red\" (chestnut) base coat. Palomino is created by a genetic mechanism of incomplete dominance, hence it is not considered true-breeding. However, most color breed registries that record palomino horses were founded before equine coat color genetics were understood as well as they are today, therefore the standard definition of a palomino is based on the visible coat color, not heritability nor the underlying presence of the dilution gene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Mare_and_foal_%28Kvetina-Marie%29.jpg/330px-Mare_and_foal_%28Kvetina-Marie%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palomino","wikidata":"Q1996502"},{"title":"Tan (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Tan is a pale tone of brown. The name is derived from tannum used in the tanning of leather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg/330px-Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://e